CAT = 'aufssLaw'; SEG = 'LAW'
Q = [
('Which body develops the Food Standards Code used across Australia?',
 'FSANZ', ['Safe Work Australia', 'The local council', 'ACCC'],
 'Food Standards Australia New Zealand (FSANZ) develops the Food Standards Code. The states and territories enforce it through their own Food Acts, often with local councils doing inspections.'),
('Who enforces food safety law in a typical café?',
 'State regulators and councils', ['FSANZ inspectors alone', "The café's own supervisor", 'The Australian Federal Police'],
 'FSANZ writes the standards but does not inspect businesses. Each state or territory food regulator, usually working with local councils, enforces them under its Food Act.'),
('Standard 3.2.2A adds three food safety management tools. Which set is correct?',
 'Supervisor, training, records', ['Supervisor, HACCP audit, licence', 'Handler training, licence, signage', 'Records, HACCP audit, signage'],
 'Standard 3.2.2A requires a certified food safety supervisor, food handler training and, for category one businesses, records or other evidence for key activities.'),
("Since when have Standard 3.2.2A's requirements applied to food businesses?",
 'December 2023', ['January 2020', 'July 2021', 'September 2025'],
 'The food safety management tools in Standard 3.2.2A applied from December 2023. They cover food service, caterers and related retail businesses.'),
("What is a 'prescribed activity' under Standard 3.2.2A?",
 'Handling unpackaged high-risk food', ['Selling any packaged food item', 'Serving alcohol with set meals', 'Delivering sealed shelf-stable goods'],
 'Prescribed activities involve handling unpackaged food that is both potentially hazardous and ready to eat. That is where the food safety risk is highest.'),
('A restaurant cooks and serves meals made from raw ingredients. What is it under Standard 3.2.2A?',
 'A category one business', ['A category two business', 'An exempt business', 'A category three business'],
 'Category one businesses, such as caterers and food service, make unpackaged potentially hazardous food into ready-to-eat food and serve it. They need all three tools, including records.'),
('A deli slices and weighs ham it bought ready to eat. What is it under Standard 3.2.2A?',
 'A category two business', ['A category one business', 'An exempt business', 'A category three business'],
 'Category two businesses handle unpackaged, ready-to-eat, potentially hazardous food made elsewhere, for example by slicing, weighing or repacking. They need a supervisor and trained handlers, but not the record-keeping tool.'),
('Which Standard 3.2.2A tool applies only to category one businesses?',
 'Keeping records', ['Appointing a supervisor', 'Training food handlers', 'Notifying the regulator'],
 'Category one and two businesses both need a food safety supervisor and trained handlers. Only category one businesses must also substantiate key activities with records or other evidence.'),
('For how long must category one businesses keep Standard 3.2.2A records?',
 'At least 3 months', ['At least 7 days', 'At least 1 month', 'At least 12 months'],
 'FSANZ guidance on the evidence tool says records must be kept for at least 3 months, so an authorised officer can check them.'),
("How recent must a food safety supervisor's certificate be under Standard 3.2.2A?",
 'Obtained within the past 5 years', ['Obtained within the past 2 years', 'Obtained within the past 10 years', 'Valid for life once obtained'],
 "The supervisor's certificate must have been obtained within the past five years, from a registered training organisation or a body the regulator recognises. After that the course must be renewed."),
("What does 'reasonably available' mean for a food safety supervisor?",
 'On site or easy to contact', ['Present at every single shift', 'Reachable by email within a week', 'Named on the menu'],
 'FSANZ says the supervisor should be on the premises or easily contactable, for example by phone, so they can advise and supervise food handlers. Someone who is never on site and never handles food is not reasonable.'),
('Which is a key duty of a food safety supervisor?',
 'Supervise food handlers', ["Approve the business's licence", 'Issue food handler certificates', "Set the council's inspection fee"],
 'The supervisor manages day-to-day food safety risks: instructing staff, checking practices and procedures, and correcting problems. Councils and training organisations handle licences and certificates.'),
('Which four areas must food handler training cover under Standard 3.2.2A?',
 'Handling, contamination, cleaning, hygiene', ['Handling, cooking, pricing, hygiene', 'Contamination, licensing, cash, hygiene', 'Cleaning, allergens, marketing, rostering'],
 'Food handlers must have skills and knowledge in safe handling of food, food contamination, cleaning and sanitising of premises and equipment, and personal hygiene.'),
('When must a new food handler have the required food safety skills and knowledge?',
 'Before handling high-risk food', ['Within 30 days of starting work', 'Within 12 months of starting', 'Only after an inspection'],
 'Training or equivalent skills must be in place before the person handles unpackaged, potentially hazardous, ready-to-eat food. There is no grace period.'),
('Does Standard 3.2.2A require every food handler to hold a certificate?',
 'No, skills and knowledge will do', ['Yes, a supervisor certificate each', 'Yes, a certificate renewed yearly', 'Only handlers under 18 years old'],
 'Food handlers must have completed a training course or have adequate skills and knowledge. A formal certificate is required only for the food safety supervisor.'),
('Before a new food business starts trading, what must it do?',
 'Notify the regulator', ['Apply to FSANZ for approval', 'Pass an HACCP audit first', 'Train all staff as supervisors'],
 'Standard 3.2.2 requires a food business to notify the relevant enforcement agency of its details before it starts. In practice this is registration with the council or state regulator; the process varies by state.'),
('What is a food safety program based on?',
 'Hazard analysis and control points', ['Customer feedback and complaints', "The council's past inspection results", 'Staff rosters and wage records'],
 'A food safety program identifies hazards, sets critical control points and limits, monitors them and records corrective action. This is the HACCP approach.'),
('In HACCP, what is a critical control point?',
 'A step where a hazard is controlled', ['Any step that takes over an hour', 'The point of sale to the customer', 'A place where staff sign in and out'],
 'A critical control point is a step where control is essential to prevent, remove or reduce a hazard to a safe level, such as cooking or cooling.'),
('A probe shows chicken in the bain-marie at 52°C. In HACCP terms, what is needed?',
 'Corrective action', ['Verification only', 'A new hazard analysis', 'No action yet'],
 'The critical limit for hot holding is 60°C or above, so this is a deviation. The corrective action, based on how long it has been out, must be taken and recorded.'),
('An authorised officer finds a serious breach. What can they issue?',
 'An improvement notice', ['A food handler licence', 'A refund order for customers', 'A training certificate'],
 'Authorised officers can issue improvement notices, prohibition orders or penalty notices, and can seize unsafe food. The exact powers and names vary by state.'),
('Under state Food Acts, what is a serious offence for a business?',
 'Selling unsafe food', ['Selling food below cost', 'Changing the menu often', 'Opening on public holidays'],
 'Every state Food Act makes it an offence to handle or sell food in a way that makes it unsafe, or to sell unsafe food. Penalties are higher when harm is likely.'),
("What is 'unsuitable' food under Australian food law?",
 'Spoiled or contaminated food', ['Food that is out of season', 'Food a customer did not order', 'Food sold at a discount price'],
 'Food is unsuitable if it is damaged, deteriorated or perished, or contains foreign matter, even if it might not make someone ill. Unsafe food is food likely to cause harm.'),
('In NSW, what extra topics must a food safety supervisor course cover?',
 'State key focus areas', ['Liquor licensing laws', 'Workplace bullying rules', 'Local parking rules'],
 'The NSW Food Authority sets key focus areas for its supervisor course: allergen management, safe egg handling, cleaning and sanitising, and Food Act offences. Other states set their own requirements.'),
("Which is one of the NSW Food Authority's key focus areas for supervisors?",
 'Safe egg handling', ['Wine and beer service', 'Barista coffee skills', 'Cash handling'],
 'The NSW key focus areas are allergen management, safe egg handling, cleaning and sanitising, and Food Act offences.'),
("In NSW, where must a copy of the food safety supervisor's certificate be kept?",
 'On the premises', ['At the council office', 'With the training provider', "At the owner's home"],
 "NSW requires a copy of the certificate to be kept on the premises, ready to show an authorised officer. Nationally, FSANZ only says the supervisor should be able to show it on request; other states set their own rules."),
('Who is responsible for making sure food handlers are trained?',
 'The food business', ['Each food handler alone', 'The local council', 'FSANZ'],
 'The duty sits with the food business: it must make sure handlers have the required skills and knowledge before they handle high-risk food. The supervisor helps deliver and check it.'),
("A café's supervisor leaves. What should the business do?",
 'Appoint a new certified supervisor', ['Wait for the next inspection', 'Let a trainee act unofficially', 'Close until the council next visits'],
 'The business must have a certified supervisor for its prescribed activities, so it needs to appoint a replacement promptly. How quickly, and whether the council must be told, varies by state.'),
("Who runs public hygiene rating schemes such as NSW's Scores on Doors?",
 'State agencies and councils', ['FSANZ and the Health Minister', 'The ACCC and Fair Trading', 'Industry and chef associations'],
 "Rating and publication schemes are run by states and councils, such as NSW's Scores on Doors and its penalty notice register. There is no single national scheme."),
]
